🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘁𝘂𝗰𝗸 𝗶𝗻 𝗧𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗶𝗻𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗙𝗶𝘅 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

A Kubernetes Pod remains in "Terminating" state for several minutes, and a new Pod is not becoming available. How would you troubleshoot this in a production environment?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗣𝗼𝗱

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the deletion timestamp, Events, termination grace period, and finalizers.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗲𝘀

Investigate whether the application is shutting down gracefully or stuck handling active requests.

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for shutdown errors, long-running requests, or termination-handler issues.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗥𝗲𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗟𝗶𝗳𝗲𝗰𝘆𝗰𝗹𝗲 𝗛𝗼𝗼𝗸𝘀

Check the Pod specification for:

- "preStop" lifecycle hooks
- "terminationGracePeriodSeconds"
- Application shutdown behavior

A long-running or blocked "preStop" hook can delay termination.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗦𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗮𝗴𝗲 𝗛𝗲𝗮𝗹𝘁𝗵

kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pvc -n <namespace>
kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

Investigate unhealthy nodes, volume unmount problems, and storage-related delays.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗙𝗶𝗻𝗮𝗹𝗶𝘇𝗲𝗿𝘀

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml

If finalizers are present, identify which controller owns them and why cleanup has not completed. Never remove finalizers blindly, as this can leave resources behind.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗹𝗼𝗮𝗱 𝗛𝗲𝗮𝗹𝘁𝗵

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -o wide
kubectl get deployment -n <namespace>

Confirm that replacement Pods are created and become Ready.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Pod stuck in "Terminating" is not always an application issue. It can involve lifecycle hooks, finalizers, node health, or storage cleanup.

Find the reason before forcing deletion.
